{# Shared abstraction rules for playbook synthesis / refinement / merge.

   Included from synthesize_playbook_from_success.j2,
   synthesize_playbook_from_recovery.j2, refine_playbook.j2, and
   merge_playbooks.j2. The block is intentionally parameter-free so it can
   be {% include %}-ed from any of those templates without disturbing the
   StrictUndefined Jinja environment.
#}
ABSTRACTION RULES (CRITICAL — the playbook is for the fault CLASS, not for
the one incident in front of you):

A diagnostic playbook must apply to ANY future incident in the same fault
class. The reader of this playbook will be facing a DIFFERENT incident, in a
different namespace, with different deployment names, different env var
names, different port numbers, different error strings, different pod
hashes. Anything you hard-code from the current incident becomes a
miscalibration the next time the same class fires.

For every Symptoms / Triage Procedure / Verification Procedure / Required
Evidence item, write the INVARIANT PATTERN — the shape of the symptom, the
kind of check, the kind of evidence — and PARAMETERIZE the incident-specific
tokens with placeholders.

Use these placeholder forms whenever an incident-specific value would
otherwise appear inline in the procedure:
  - `<NAMESPACE>` for any Kubernetes namespace
  - `<DEPLOYMENT>`, `<POD>`, `<SERVICE>`, `<CONTAINER>` for resource names
  - `<ENV_VAR>` for environment variable names
  - `<PORT>` for port numbers
  - `<EXPECTED_VALUE>` / `<OBSERVED_VALUE>` for variable values
  - `<ERROR_STRING>` for matched error text or log line content
  - `<TARGET_SERVICE>` / `<COMPONENT>` for application-specific component names
  - `<FILE>` / `<PATH>` for file system paths
You may invent additional `<UPPER_SNAKE>` placeholders when none of the
above fit. Make the placeholder name describe the role, not the value.

NO INCIDENT-SPECIFIC TOKENS ANYWHERE IN THE DOCUMENT. The playbook must NOT
mention any of the following from the current incident:
  - specific namespace names (e.g. `astronomy-shop`)
  - specific deployment / pod / service / container names (e.g. `frontend`)
  - specific environment variable names (e.g. `CHECKOUT_ADDR`)
  - specific port numbers
  - specific error strings or log line excerpts (e.g.
    `'Could not parse target name ""'`)
  - specific pod hashes or other generated identifiers
  - any incident reference / trace ID / timestamp
Do NOT smuggle these in as parenthetical "Example:" asides either. The
playbook contains the abstract pattern only; the concrete evidence lives in
the incident files, not in the playbook.

GOOD (purely abstract — would apply to any missing-env-var incident, no
matter what the variable, deployment, namespace, or error string is):
  1. **Action:** `kubectl get deployment <DEPLOYMENT> -n <NAMESPACE> -o yaml`
     **Expected shape:** an environment variable `<ENV_VAR>` that the
     application requires (typically resolving to the address of a
     downstream `<TARGET_SERVICE>`) is absent from
     `spec.template.spec.containers[*].env`.
     **If absent:** the env var is set; the issue is elsewhere — check for
     wrong value, wrong host, network policy, or a different fault class.

BAD (incident-specific — will not generalize to the next fault in the
class, even though every named token is real):
  1. **Action:** `kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o yaml`
     **Expected shape:** `CHECKOUT_ADDR` is missing from
     `spec.template.spec.containers[0].env`.
     **If absent:** investigate other application-level errors.

The same rule applies to:
  - **Symptoms:** describe the SHAPE of the symptom (e.g. "application logs
    contain an error showing an empty/null reference where a configured
    address should be"), not the literal error string or the literal pod
    name.
  - **Required Evidence checkboxes:** describe the kind of check
    ("Confirmed an env var the application reads at startup is absent from
    the failing Deployment's container env spec"), not the exact variable
    or resource name. The checker will instantiate `<ENV_VAR>` and
    `<DEPLOYMENT>` from the incident under investigation.
  - **Known Distractors and Failure Patterns:** describe the misleading
    pattern in general terms; parameterize the incident-specific tokens.

This abstraction rule does NOT relax the ban on vague phrases listed in the
constraints below. You must write PARAMETERIZED-but-precise content, not
woolly content. `<ENV_VAR>` and `<NAMESPACE>` are parameterized and
precise; "the relevant configuration" and "appropriate values" are vague.
The former is required; the latter is forbidden.
